      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Three inpaints, two OPPOSITE mask conventions - read this before wiring one\n\ncv2.inpaint (photo module), cv2.xphoto.inpaint (contrib) and cv2.ft.inpaint (fuzzy F-transform, contrib) all do the same job and take a mask with the SAME name and OPPOSITE meanings:\n\n    cv2.inpaint          mask non-zero = the HOLE  (repair these pixels)\n    cv2.xphoto.inpaint   mask non-zero = KNOWN     (trust these pixels)\n    cv2.ft.inpaint       mask non-zero = KNOWN     (trust these pixels, same as xphoto)\n\nSo the SAME mask wired into all three repairs the subject in cv2.inpaint and repairs everything EXCEPT the subject in the other two. That is why they are separate nodes with the module kept in the id, and why 'cv2.bitwise_not' sits between the shared mask and the xphoto/ft branches here. The nodes' tooltips say so too, and the three previews here show it directly.\n\nALGORITHMS: cv2.inpaint offers TELEA (fast marching) and NS (Navier-Stokes); both diffuse surrounding colour inwards and blur across large holes. xphoto adds FSR_FAST / FSR_BEST (frequency-selective extrapolation, which reconstructs TEXTURE and periodic structure rather than smearing) and SHIFTMAP (patch offsets, best for repeating content). FSR is much slower but is the one to reach for on a textured background. ft (the fuzzy F-transform) rebuilds missing structure with ONE_STEP / MULTI_STEP / ITERATIVE refinement. \n\nVERIFYING THE REPAIR: 'CV Image Hash Compare' (cv2.img_hash) checks the result still LOOKS like the original. Careful: cv2's own compare() is inconsistent - most algorithms return a distance (0 = identical) but RadialVarianceHash returns a SIMILARITY (1.0 = identical). The node normalises every algorithm to '0 = identical', so 'distance <= threshold' always means 'similar'."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
